A||N||15||cscougar||Bulldog Remote Entry Kit||||1||
Z||000000||cscougar||10-30-2000||02:40 PM||||At Sam's Wholesale Club, they are selling a Remote Entry Kit that would work on my Fiero for $29.99.  Has anyone tried the Bulldog Security Brand remote entry kit? If so, how hard is it to wire in?<P>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||TheFiero64||10-30-2000||03:16 PM||||I have a Bulldog remote car starter. The directions were easy and detailed <B>step by step</B>. That sounds like a great price. I got my car starter from Ebay for $45.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||cscougar||10-30-2000||04:17 PM||||This is a remote car entry system.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||10-30-2000||04:59 PM||||I know that. I was just telling you that bulldog has good directions with there products. The car starter also has keyless entry built in. In the instructions it tells you what wires to tap into. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||cscougar||10-30-2000||05:22 PM||||Thanks....I went to their web page and downloaded instructions and manuals on the installation and operation.  Looks like you can configure it a bunch of different ways.  I think I will pick up one and try to install on my 85 GT and let the forum know what comes of it.  It is a good price for that option.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2xsess||10-30-2000||06:20 PM||||Yes please keep us posted, I've been thinking of getting a keyless entry setup.<BR>And $30 is a great price.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT<p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 10-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hank is Here||10-30-2000||08:51 PM||||Is it necessary to cut any wires to add the remote starter to the car, or could it be done without cutting?  On other cars I have owned wires added by previous owners caused problems.  <P>Mark Johnson ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||10-30-2000||09:16 PM||||No, you do not have to cut wires to add remote starter. It is fairly eaisy if you ask me. I can tell you what all you need to hook to and which wires to splice. Email me if ya need help installing it. Just remember to hook all the power wires to differnt power inputs. The remote starter should have about 3 or 4 heavy guage power wires needing to be hooked up, be sure, like the insturutions say, to hook each one to a different power wire comming from the car. The power wire going to the brain unit can be hooked up useing a wire you have already hooked the starter block to.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||10-30-2000||09:22 PM||||When I bought my ('88) Formula, it came with very few options. It had W66, a/c, and sunroof, as the only options on it. I dug in, added PW, PL, and PM. I then wired eveything into the existing alarm system, and relocated the "brain". I can't express how nice keyless entry is, and how easy it it to wire to a keyless entry unit. All I needed to dig out were the blue and orange wires from a door lock switch, and splice into them. Might be different for others? I would definitely add it for $30, that is a good deal, and a very useable addition.||||reg||1
Z||000009||cscougar||10-30-2000||09:55 PM||||If you want to know how to wire the Bulldog<BR>Door Openers, you can go to <A HREF="http://www.bulldogsecurity.com" TARGET=_blank>www.bulldogsecurity.com</A>  and download the instructions and manuals.  I am going to pick one up tomorrow and connect it.  The wiring doesn't look too complicated.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||10-30-2000||10:10 PM||||Keyless entry is as easy as 1, 2, 3. But the remote starting get a little more complicated. If anyone decides to do keyless entry, when you first test it, be sure to stand ready to pull the plug. I have seen more then a few people hook them up wrong, and the starter enguages while the car is already running, not a good thing.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000011||TheFiero64||10-31-2000||10:41 AM||||I would never cut into the wires. I used little clips that just put a pin-hole in the wires and you plug the harness from the starter into them.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000012||Seanh||10-31-2000||04:53 PM||||If you do decide to do this, the conectors you probally want are called t-tap conectors. They work great for doing this. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||286SE'S||11-01-2000||03:50 PM||||Does the remote starter come with a big lead foot to put on the clutch pedal to enable the starter? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||11-01-2000||04:59 PM||||Theres not chance the Sams club system is going to work with a manual tranny. The system I have (made by Firsttech) will work will all kinds of cars. It will work with manual and carbed engines as well as desiel. It was pretty pricy, but I love that thing. It will start the car from a mile away. It also has a display screen showing you the status of the car at all times. Its really cool.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero56||11-01-2000||06:16 PM||||Most any system will work with manual tranny. Always park the car in neutral, always use the parking brake, and by-pass the neutral start switch so the starter will enguage even without the clutch depressed. Not that I recommend this, but anything is possible.||||reg||1
